/// Chapter 15 of the guide, written from md-docs/15-styling.md by
/// scripts/site-from-guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page StylingGuide(path: "/docs/guide/styling", title: "Styling", description: "Themes and tokens, style blocks with live values, nested rules and media queries, transitions, classes and stylesheets, dark mode, responsive layout.", layout: DocsShell("Styling", chapter: t("guide.chapter", { n: "15" }), blurb: "Restyle the whole site with a theme, one element with a style block, and many elements with your own stylesheet — and make the layout responsive.", source: "md-docs/15-styling.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "before-you-start", label: "Before you start"), Anchor(id: "change-the-whole-site-with-a-theme", label: "Change the whole site with a theme"), Anchor(id: "style-one-element", label: "Style one element"), Anchor(id: "animate-a-style-change", label: "Animate a style change"), Anchor(id: "classes-and-your-own-stylesheet", label: "Classes and your own stylesheet"), Anchor(id: "override-a-built-in-s-look", label: "Override a built-in's look"), Anchor(id: "style-a-component-s-variants", label: "Style a component's variants"), Anchor(id: "dark-mode", label: "Dark mode"), Anchor(id: "lay-out-a-page-without-css", label: "Lay out a page without CSS"), Anchor(id: "responsive-values", label: "Responsive values"), Anchor(id: "find-your-styles-in-the-build-output", label: "Find your styles in the build output"), Anchor(id: "troubleshooting", label: "Troubleshooting")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/expressions", prevLabel: "14. {t("ch.14")}", nextTo: "/docs/guide/forms", nextLabel: "16. {t("ch.16")}")) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("This chapter shows you how to change the way your site looks, from the whole site down to one element. Every built-in element already ships styled, so you only write styles for what you want to change.\n\nYou will be able to:\n\n- change colours, fonts and spacing across the site with a `theme`;\n- style one element with a `style \{ \}` block, including live values, hover states and media queries;\n- share rules between elements with classes and your own `.css` files;\n- add dark mode;\n- make a layout change with the width of the screen.")

        Heading("Before you start", id: "before-you-start").h2

        Markdown("You need a project that builds (see [Getting started](/docs/guide/getting-started)) and you should know how to place elements and pass them props ([Elements](/docs/guide/elements)). Styles that follow state use `state` values ([State and reactivity](/docs/guide/state)).\n\nThree tools cover almost everything. Pick the narrowest one that does the job:")

        Table(caption: "Before you start") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("To change…").header
                    Table.Cell("Use").header
                    Table.Cell("Section").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("The whole site: every button, card and link")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("a `theme`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Change the whole site with a theme](#change-the-whole-site-with-a-theme)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("One element")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("a `style \{ \}` block on it") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Style one element](#style-one-element)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("A group of elements, or something a block cannot say")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("a class and a `.css` file") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Classes and your own stylesheet](#classes-and-your-own-stylesheet)") }
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("Change the whole site with a theme", id: "change-the-whole-site-with-a-theme").h2

        Markdown("To change the colours, fonts, spacing or corner radius of everything at once, declare a `theme`. A theme sets *design tokens* — named values such as `color-primary` or `radius-md` that every built-in reads.\n\nThis theme gives the site a teal brand colour, the Inter font and rounder corners:")

        CodeBlock(#"theme Brand {
    color-primary: #0F766E
    color-secondary: #134E4A
    color-background: #FFFFFF
    color-surface: #F8FAFC
    color-text: #0F172A
    color-text-muted: #64748B
    color-border: #E2E8F0
    font-family: 'Inter', system-ui, sans-serif
    font-family-mono: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace
    radius-md: 14px
    spacing-lg: 2.5rem
    shadow-md: 0 4px 12px rgba(0,0,0,0.08)
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Every primary button, link and focus ring now uses `#0F766E`, because the built-ins read `color-primary` rather than a fixed colour.\n\nHow a theme works:\n\n- Each value is raw CSS, to the end of its line. Do not quote it.\n- You list only the tokens you change. Every token you leave out keeps its *baseline* value — the default the compiler ships.\n- The baseline has 71 tokens, in groups: colour, font, spacing, radius, shadow, motion, easing, breakpoints, code and layout. [Design tokens](/docs/guide/tokens) lists each one with its baseline value.\n- Every token becomes a CSS custom property on `:root` (`--color-primary`). You read it from a style block as `$color-primary` and from a stylesheet as `var(--color-primary)`.\n- A theme may add tokens of its own, such as `surface-raised: #131519`. They become custom properties like the rest.\n- The breakpoints are tokens too, so moving one moves every media query the build writes (see [Responsive values](#responsive-values)).\n\nIf the project declares one theme, the build uses it. If it declares several, choose one in `webfluent.app.json`:")

        CodeBlock(#"{ "theme": { "name": "Brand" } }"#, title: "json")

        Markdown("The contrast lint, `A13`, checks the text and background pairs of your theme and warns when one falls below the WCAG AA contrast ratio. Run `wf theme` to see every token, where it comes from and which pairs it checked.\n\nTo keep the built-ins' layout and mechanics but drop their look — for a site that brings its whole design — set `theme.builtin` in `webfluent.app.json`:")

        Table(caption: "Change the whole site with a theme") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell.header { Markdown("`theme.builtin`") }
                    Table.Cell("What the build ships for each built-in you use").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`\"full\"` (default)") }
                    Table.Cell("Its layout, its mechanics and its baseline look")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`\"structural\"`") }
                    Table.Cell("Only its layout and mechanics: focus rings, overlays, the grid")
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("Style one element", id: "style-one-element").h2

        Markdown("To change one element, give it a `style \{ \}` block. The block holds CSS declarations, and they apply to that element only.\n\nThis card uses tokens for its spacing and colours, a live value for its accent border and width, a hover rule, a rule for its paragraphs and a media query:")

        CodeBlock(##"page Styled(path: "/styled", title: "Styled card", description: "A card with its own style.") {
    state accent = "#D97706"
    state wide = false

    Heading("Styled card").h1
    Card {
        style {
            padding: $lg                       // spacing-lg, from the property
            background: $surface              // color-surface
            border: 1px solid $border
            border-radius: $radius-lg
            box-shadow: $shadow-md
            border-left: 4px solid {accent}    // repaints when `accent` changes
            max-width: {if wide { "100%" } else { "40rem" }}
            &:hover { box-shadow: $shadow-lg }
            & > p { margin: 0 }
            @media (max-width: 640px) { padding: $sm }
        }
        Text("Hover me, or widen me.")
        Button("Widen") { on click { wide = !wide } }
        Button("Make it blue") { on click { accent = "#2563EB" } }
    }
}"##, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The card gets a deeper shadow on hover. **Widen** lets it fill the page, and **Make it blue** recolours its left border at once.")

        Heading("Write declarations", id: "write-declarations").h3

        Markdown("- Write one declaration per line, or several on one line separated by `;`.\n- Write values as CSS, with no quotes: `padding: 2rem`, not `\"2rem\"`.\n- Never separate two declarations with a comma. `width: 2rem, flex: none` is `E002`, because a style block is CSS, not a map.")

        Heading("Use a token", id: "use-a-token").h3

        Markdown("`$name` reads a design token. A full name, such as `$color-primary` or `$surface-raised`, works on any property. A short name is resolved by the property it is written on:")

        Table(caption: "Use a token") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("You write").header
                    Table.Cell("It means").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`padding: $lg` (and `margin`, `gap`, `width`, `height`, `top` …)") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`spacing-lg`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`color: $primary`, `background: $surface`, `border: 1px solid $border`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`color-primary`, `color-surface`, `color-border`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`border-radius: $lg`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`radius-lg`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`box-shadow: $md`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`shadow-md`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`font-size: $xl`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`font-size-xl`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`transition: $fast`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`transition-fast`") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("A token that the theme does not declare is `V06`, an error, because the browser would drop the declaration. A property name that CSS does not have, such as `colr:`, is `V05`, a warning that suggests the nearest property.")

        Heading("Splice a live value", id: "splice-a-live-value").h3

        Markdown("`\{expr\}` puts a value into a declaration. When the value reads state, the declaration updates whenever that state changes. A splice may be the whole value (`\{accent\}`) or part of one (`\{pct\}%`).\n\nA splice that is a bare number for a length, such as `width: \{pct\}`, is `V07`: the browser drops a length with no unit. Write `width: \{pct\}%` or `width: \{pct\}px`.")

        Heading("Style a state with a nested rule", id: "style-a-state-with-a-nested-rule").h3

        Markdown("A nested rule styles the element while it is in some state. `&` is the element itself, as in CSS nesting:\n\n- `&:hover`, `&:focus-visible` (the keyboard focus ring), `&:active`, `&:disabled`, `&::placeholder`, `&:focus-within`;\n- `&.active`, `& > p`;\n- the ARIA states a control keeps in its attributes: `&[aria-current=\"page\"]`, which the router sets on the current `Link`, and `&[aria-pressed=\"true\"]`, `&[aria-selected=\"true\"]`, `&[aria-checked=\"true\"]`, `&[aria-expanded=\"true\"]`, `&[aria-invalid=\"true\"]`.\n\n`@media (…) \{ \}` and `@container (…) \{ \}` nest the same way.\n\nA nested rule is compiled into the stylesheet, so its values must be known at build time: literals and tokens, not state. To make a hover colour follow state, set a custom property on the element and read it in the rule:")

        CodeBlock(##"page Swatch(path: "/swatch", title: "Swatch", description: "A hover colour that follows state.") {
    state hoverColor = "#0F766E"

    Heading("Swatch").h1
    Button("Hover me") {
        style {
            --hover-bg: {hoverColor}
            &:hover { background: var(--hover-bg) }
        }
    }
    Button("Use amber") { on click { hoverColor = "#D97706" } }
}"##, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Read your own custom property with `var(--name)`. `$name` is only for design tokens.")

        Heading("How the build compiles a style block", id: "how-the-build-compiles-a-style-block").h3

        Markdown("- The parts of a block that do not read state become a stylesheet rule, under a class named by the block's content. The element carries the class, so a hundred identical cards share one rule and the pre-rendered page has it before any script runs.\n- A declaration that reads state is set on the element by the runtime and updated when the state changes.\n- Nested state rules and media rules are marked `!important`, so they win over the element's base declarations while they apply. When a state rule and a media rule set the same property, the media rule wins.")

        Heading("Animate a style change", id: "animate-a-style-change").h2

        Markdown("To make a property change smoothly instead of jumping, add a `transition \{ \}` block. Each line names a property, a duration and an optional easing.\n\nThis card fades and grows when you press **Toggle**:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Fade(path: "/fade", title: "Fade", description: "A card that fades in and out.") {
    state active = false

    Heading("Fade").h1
    Card {
        style { opacity: {if active { 1 } else { 0.4 }}; transform: {if active { "none" } else { "scale(0.98)" }} }
        transition { opacity: $fast; transform: 200ms ease-out }
        Text("Fades")
    }
    Button("Toggle") { on click { active = !active } }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("A duration may be a token — `$fast`, `$normal` or `$slow` — or a raw value such as `200ms ease-out`. A reader who asked their system for less motion gets no transition. Enter and exit animations, and other motion, are in [Motion](/docs/guide/motion).")

        Heading("Classes and your own stylesheet", id: "classes-and-your-own-stylesheet").h2

        Markdown("To share a rule between many elements, or to write what a `style \{ \}` block cannot say, put it in a `.css` file and add its class with `class:`.\n\nUse a stylesheet for:\n\n- a selector over several elements;\n- a `@keyframes` or a `@font-face`;\n- a class that a dozen elements share by name;\n- a rule on `html`, `body` or `::selection`.")

        Heading("Add a stylesheet", id: "add-a-stylesheet").h3

        Markdown("Every `.css` file anywhere under `src/` is part of the build. You can keep one global file, or put a file beside the component it styles:")

        CodeBlock(#"src/
├── styles.css            # global rules
└── components/
    ├── Card.wf
    └── card.css          # beside the component it styles"#, title: "text")

        CodeBlock(#"/* src/components/card.css */
.feature {
  container-type: inline-size;
}
.feature--first::before {
  content: counter(feature, upper-roman);
  color: var(--color-text-muted);
}"#, title: "css")

        Markdown("The build reads the files in path order, minifies them and bundles them into `styles.css`. `var(--token)` reaches every design token. There is no scoping: a class is global, so name it on purpose.\n\nTo link a sheet the build does not compile — a file in `public/` or a URL — list it in `meta.stylesheets` in `webfluent.app.json`. It is linked ahead of `styles.css`.")

        Heading("Add a class", id: "add-a-class").h3

        Markdown("`class: \"name\"` adds a class to the element. It does not replace the element's own classes: `Container(class: \"hero\")` renders `class=\"wf-container hero\"`.\n\nThe value may be an expression, so a class can follow state:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Welcome(path: "/welcome", title: "Welcome", description: "A heading whose class follows state.") {
    state active = true

    Container(class: "hero") {
        Heading("Welcome", class: if active { "title is-on" } else { "title" }).h1
        Button("Switch") { on click { active = !active } }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("When several classes each have their own condition, pass a map or a list:\n\n- In a **map**, each key is a class, and it is on while its value is true.\n- A **list** joins strings and maps, and skips anything that is `null`.")

        CodeBlock(#"page Chores(path: "/chores", title: "Chores", description: "Classes that follow state.") {
    state done = false
    state urgent = true
    state tone = "calm"

    Heading("Chores").h1
    Text("Water the plants", class: { "is-done": done, "is-urgent": urgent })
    Container(class: ["panel", tone, if urgent { "is-loud" }]) { Text("Due today") }
    Button("Done") { on click { done = !done } }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The rules for a class value:\n\n- A map's values must be conditions (`Bool`).\n- A bare condition is not a class: `class: active` is `T01`. Write `\{ \"is-active\": active \}`, which says what `active` turns on.\n- When the value changes, only the classes that `class:` itself named come off. A class a script added with `classList.add` stays.")

        Heading("Target the built-ins", id: "target-the-built-ins").h3

        Markdown("Each built-in wears `wf-*` classes, such as `wf-btn`, `wf-btn--primary` and `wf-card`. They are stable across releases, so a stylesheet may target them.\n\nDo not add another built-in's class to an element with `class:`. It brings that built-in's rules with it, and draws `V04`.")

        Heading("Override a built-in's look", id: "override-a-built-in-s-look").h2

        Markdown("To decide which rule wins, know the three layers of CSS on a page. From weakest to strongest:\n\n1. **The built-ins' own rules** (`.wf-btn`, `.wf-card`), shipped in `styles.css`.\n2. **Your stylesheets** (`src/**/*.css`), bundled after them. On equal specificity, yours win.\n3. **`style \{ \}` blocks**, compiled to rules with tripled specificity, with nested state and media rules marked `!important`. An element's own block beats any stylesheet, as the inline style it replaces would.\n\nSo choose the tool by how far the change reaches:\n\n- To restyle **one element**, use its `style \{ \}` block.\n- To restyle **every button**, write `.wf-btn \{ … \}` in a stylesheet, or change the tokens in a theme. Because the built-ins read tokens, `color-primary` recolours every primary button, link and focus ring at once.\n- To drop the built-ins' look entirely, set `theme.builtin: \"structural\"` (see [Change the whole site with a theme](#change-the-whole-site-with-a-theme)).")

        Heading("Style a component's variants", id: "style-a-component-s-variants").h2

        Markdown("To style each variant of your own component from a stylesheet, use its enum props. A component writes each enum prop to its root element as a `data-<prop>` attribute, such as `data-tone=\"loud\"`, so one rule styles one variant ([Components](/docs/guide/components#enum-props)):")

        CodeBlock(#".note[data-tone="loud"] { font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-danger) }"#, title: "css")

        Heading("Dark mode", id: "dark-mode").h2

        Markdown("To add dark mode, declare a second theme with the tokens that change, and name both themes in `webfluent.app.json`:")

        CodeBlock(#"{ "theme": { "name": "Light", "dark": "Night" } }"#, title: "json")

        Markdown("The dark theme's tokens replace the light theme's in two cases:\n\n- when the reader's system prefers dark (`prefers-color-scheme: dark`) and the reader has not chosen light;\n- whenever the reader chose dark with `setTheme(\"dark\")`.\n\nThis page declares both themes and lets the reader choose:")

        CodeBlock(#"theme Light {
    color-background: #FFFFFF
    color-text: #0F172A
}

theme Night {
    color-background: #0B1220
    color-surface: #111827
    color-text: #E5E7EB
    color-text-muted: #9CA3AF
    color-border: #1F2937
}

page Prefs(path: "/prefs", title: "Appearance", description: "Choose light or dark.") {
    Heading("Appearance").h1
    Text("You are on the {theme} theme")
    Row(gap: .sm) {
        Button("Light") { on click { setTheme("light") } }
        Button("Dark") { on click { setTheme("dark") } }
        Button("System") { on click { setTheme("system") } }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The text under the heading reads `You are on the dark theme` after you press **Dark**.\n\n- `setTheme(\"light\")` forces light, `setTheme(\"dark\")` forces dark, and `setTheme(\"system\")` follows the system again.\n- `theme` reads the reader's choice: `\"light\"`, `\"dark\"` or `\"system\"`.\n- The choice is kept in the browser's storage across visits. The page's script applies it before it draws the page. The system preference needs no script: it is a media query in the stylesheet, so it applies from the first paint.")

        Callout("Only what reads tokens switches with the theme. A colour written raw, such as `background: #FFFFFF`, stays the same in dark mode. Use tokens such as `$surface` and `$text` for anything that should change.", kind: .warning)

        Heading("Lay out a page without CSS", id: "lay-out-a-page-without-css").h2

        Markdown("Most layout needs no `style` at all. `Stack`, `Row`, `Grid`, `Container`, `Spacer` and `Divider` take `gap:`, `align:`, `justify:` and `columns:` props ([Elements](/docs/guide/elements#layout-elements)).\n\nThis page puts a title and a button on one line, with a grid of cards below that has one, two or three columns depending on the screen:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Layout(path: "/projects", title: "Projects", description: "A grid of projects.") {
    Container {
        Stack(gap: .lg) {
            Row(justify: .between, align: .center) {
                Heading("Projects").h1
                Button("New").primary.sm
            }
            Grid(columns: { base: 1, md: 2, lg: 3 }, gap: .md) {
                for n in 1..=6 { Card { Text("Project {n}") } }
            }
        }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Heading("Responsive values", id: "responsive-values").h2

        Markdown("To make a layout prop change with the screen width, give it one value per breakpoint instead of one value:")

        CodeBlock(#"Grid(columns: { base: 1, md: 2, lg: 3 }, gap: { base: .sm, lg: .lg }) { … }
Row(direction: { base: .column, md: .row }) { … }
Column(span: { base: 12, md: 6 }) { … }"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The build compiles each step to a class and a media query. No JavaScript runs and no resize listener is added, so the first paint already has the right layout. A `viewport` test, by contrast, paints one branch and swaps it after the page's script starts.\n\n- The steps are `base`, `sm`, `md`, `lg` and `xl`.\n- A step you leave out keeps the value of the step below it, so `\{ base: 1, lg: 3 \}` is one column until `lg`.\n- Each step is a design token, `screen-sm` to `screen-xl`. Move one in your theme and every media query moves with it, `viewport.md` included:")

        CodeBlock(#"theme Brand { screen-md: 900px }"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The same names work inside a `style \{ \}` block: `@md \{ … \}` means `@media (min-width: $screen-md)`.")

        CodeBlock(#"Heading("Title").h1 {
    style {
        font-size: 24px
        @md { font-size: 34px }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Heading("Make a layout reflow on a narrow screen", id: "make-a-layout-reflow-on-a-narrow-screen").h3

        Markdown("A `Row`, `Grid` or `Column` stays as you write it at every width. To make one reflow on a narrow screen, add the `.stacks` flag, or say what it does at each width with a responsive value:")

        CodeBlock(#"Row.stacks { … }                          // a column on a narrow screen
Grid.stacks { … }                         // one column on a narrow screen
Grid(columns: { base: 1, md: 3 }) { … }   // or say what it does at each width"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("What `.stacks` does:")

        Table(caption: "Make a layout reflow on a narrow screen") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Element").header
                    Table.Cell("Below 1024px").header
                    Table.Cell("Below 768px").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Row.stacks`") }
                    Table.Cell("unchanged")
                    Table.Cell("a column")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Grid.stacks`") }
                    Table.Cell("two columns")
                    Table.Cell("one column")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Column.stacks`") }
                    Table.Cell("half width, when its span is 3 or 4")
                    Table.Cell("full width")
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("Older versions of the engine reflowed every `Row` and `Grid` this way below 768px, with `!important`, whether you asked or not. They no longer do. `wf migrate` adds `.stacks` to every `Row`, `Grid` and `Column` of a WebFluent 2 project, so the old behaviour carries over, written where you can see and change it.\n\nKeep `viewport` ([State and reactivity](/docs/guide/state#the-browser-as-values)) for what changes behaviour, such as a drawer instead of a sidebar. It follows `matchMedia`, which fires once when the answer changes, not on every pixel of a drag.")

        Heading("Find your styles in the build output", id: "find-your-styles-in-the-build-output").h2

        Markdown("The build writes your styles to two places:\n\n- `styles.css` holds the tokens something in the output uses (`:root \{ --color-primary: … \}`), the dark theme's overrides, the rules of the built-ins you used (and only those), your bundled stylesheets, and the compiled `style \{ \}` rules that more than one page reaches.\n- `pages/<Name>.css` holds the `style \{ \}` rules that only one page can reach. The page loads it after `styles.css`.\n\n[Performance](/docs/guide/performance) shows how to see what each file weighs.")

        Heading("Troubleshooting", id: "troubleshooting").h2

        Table(caption: "Troubleshooting") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("What you see").header
                    Table.Cell("Why, and what to do").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`E002`: a `,` after the value of a property") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Declarations are separated by a new line or `;`, never `,`.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`V06`: a token the theme does not declare") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Check the token's spelling against `wf theme`, or declare it in your theme.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`V05`: a property CSS does not have") }
                    Table.Cell("Fix the property name; the warning names the nearest one.")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`V07`: a bare number spliced as a length") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Add the unit: `width: \{pct\}%`.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("A hover rule ignores a state value")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Nested rules are compiled at build time. Set a custom property from state and read it with `var(--…)` in the rule.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Your stylesheet rule loses to an element")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The element's `style \{ \}` block is stronger than any stylesheet. Move the rule into the block, or remove the declaration from the block.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Part of the page stays light in dark mode")
                    Table.Cell("That part uses a raw colour. Replace it with a token.")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`V04`: a `wf-*` class in `class:`") }
                    Table.Cell("Give your element its own class instead of a built-in's.")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A `Row` or `Grid` does not reflow on a phone") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Add `.stacks`, or give the prop a responsive value.") }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
